{"ts": 1789778019.172467, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Evaluations are deterministic (identical repeats), so all spread across specs is real in-sample structure \u2014 the question is purely which part generalizes.\n\nMy decision: **submit the 2-feature pair {18, 15}, both long**, not the higher-scoring triple.\n\nReasoning:\n- **18+15 is the genuine signal.** Both are individually strong (1.187, 0.673) and combine to 1.309 \u2014 more than either alone, so they carry partly independent information and diversify.\n- **Feature 12 adds +0.018 (1.309 \u2192 1.327), which is noise.** The Sharpe standard error here is ~0.22. A gain of 0.018 is under a tenth of that. Meanwhile 12 adds a third estimated sign and a whole extra feature's idiosyncratic risk. Note also that 10 *other* third features all *reduced* the Sharpe (1.169\u20131.288) \u2014 the distribution of third-feature effects is centered below the pair, so 12 being marginally on top looks like the max of ~38 noisy draws, exactly the selection artifact that vanishes out-of-sample.\n- The bar of 1.016 was computed against my 1.327 best; the pair at 1.309 still clears it comfortably without the overfitting.\n\nFor my OOS belief: I shrink meaningfully from 1.309. I searched all 40 singles, 6 pairs, and ~14 triples (~60 evaluations), so even my \"winner\" carries selection bias \u2014 though feature 18's standalone 1.187 was found on the very first unbiased sweep, which is reassuring and not a cherry-pick. Shrinking for selection plus expected in-sample-to-OOS decay (regime drift, unmodeled costs), I center at **1.05** with **sd 0.32** \u2014 wide enough to cover a real chance of landing near 0.4 if the relationship is unstable, or near 1.4 if it holds."}
